course: everyday
lesson: 419
title: Homework first?
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Kai did his spelling homework in four minutes and fifty seconds, a personal record. He spelt «because» three different ways on one page and wrote «Wednesday» as «Wensday». Then he played Roblox and felt very clever. On Thursday Mrs Okafor handed it back with «Please do this again, properly» in green pen, and Kai spent Thursday's game time redoing it. Rushed homework comes back to you, usually on the day you least want it.
explain: Priya tried the opposite: «Games first, homework after, I've got loads of time.» By eight o'clock she was yawning over her fractions, got four of them wrong and cried a bit into her pencil case. Leo tried a third way: homework first, then Minecraft as a reward. He finished faster, because the reward was waiting, and enjoyed the game more, because nothing was nagging at the back of his head.
explain: Distractions were the real enemy. Theo's phone buzzed every two minutes with his friends arguing about the GTA 6 trailer, a game rated 18 that none of them was even allowed to play. He put the phone in the hall and finished in twenty minutes instead of an hour. Ruby's mum has a rule that homework is only done when she has checked it; Ruby rolled her eyes, but her mum found two mistakes, Ruby fixed them, and the next day she got full marks. Jayden forgot his homework completely after a big game night, owned up to his teacher, did it at lunch and missed playtime.
explain: Games and homework are not always enemies. Ollie learnt his seven and eight times tables from a maths game and got them all right in the test, to his own surprise. Mia used a game set in ancient Egypt to start her history project and then checked every fact in library books, because games change history to make it exciting. Zara does her reading on the bus so she can play when she gets home. And Noah asked his dad for help with long division; they finished early and played a family game of Mario Kart, which his dad lost loudly.
fact: Homework done in a rush is often full of mistakes and may have to be done again.
fact: Doing homework first and playing after usually works better, because people are less tired and nothing is left hanging.
fact: A game waiting as a reward can help you finish work faster.
fact: Phones and messages nearby make homework take much longer.
fact: Putting the phone in another room during homework is one of the simplest ways to focus.
fact: Having a grown-up check homework catches mistakes before the teacher does.
fact: Planning homework before a big game night stops it being forgotten.
fact: Telling a teacher honestly about forgotten homework is better than making up an excuse.
fact: Some games can help you practise things like times tables.
fact: Games about history often change facts to make the story exciting, so facts should be checked in good books.
fact: Asking for help when stuck is clever, not weak, and often makes homework faster.
exercise: Why did Kai have to do his spelling homework again? | He rushed it and made lots of mistakes | He did it in the wrong exercise book | His teacher lost the first copy he handed in | He forgot to write his name on it
exercise: How did Kai feel after finishing in under five minutes? | Very clever | Worried about his spelling | Tired and bored | Proud of his handwriting
exercise: What did redoing the homework cost Kai? | His game time on Thursday | His place on the football team | A note home to his parents | His break time every day for a week
exercise: What went wrong when Priya played first? | She was too tired and got fractions wrong | She forgot which game she had played | Her friends did her homework for her on the phone | She finished the homework too quickly
exercise: Why did Leo finish faster with a game as a reward? | The reward was waiting for him at the end | The game gave him the answers | His homework was much shorter than usual that day | He did only half of the homework
exercise: Why did Leo enjoy his game more after homework? | Nothing was nagging at the back of his head | The game was a brand new one | He played for longer than usual | His friends were all online then
exercise: What slowed Theo down? | His phone buzzing with friends' messages | His pencil breaking again and again | A very hard question at the end of his homework | His little brother singing loudly
exercise: What did Theo do that helped him finish in twenty minutes? | Put his phone in the hall | Turned his phone up louder | Answered every message quickly | Asked his friends to do it
exercise: Why was it funny that Theo's friends argued about GTA 6? | It is rated 18 and none of them could play it | They had all finished the whole game the day before | The trailer had not been made yet | They did not like games at all
exercise: How did Ruby feel about her mum checking her homework? | Annoyed at first | Glad from the very start | Scared her mum would shout | Proud she needed no check
exercise: What happened after Ruby's mum checked? | Ruby fixed two mistakes and got full marks | Ruby's mum did the homework herself | Ruby had to start all over again | Ruby's teacher said the checking was cheating
exercise: What did Jayden do after forgetting his homework? | Told his teacher and did it at lunch | Said his dog had eaten it on the way to school | Copied a friend's in the corridor before the bell | Pretended he had been ill the night before
exercise: What surprised Ollie? | A game helped him learn his times tables | His times tables test was cancelled | His teacher played the same game | He got every answer wrong
exercise: Why did Mia check her game's history facts in books? | Games change history to make it exciting | Books are always more fun and exciting than games | Her teacher does not allow games | The game was in a different language
exercise: What did Noah gain by asking his dad for help? | They finished early and played together | His dad did all the homework for him while he watched | He got out of doing the homework | He got extra homework from his dad
exercise: Which took a kind of courage? | Jayden owning up about forgotten homework | Kai finishing his homework in record time | Theo leaving his phone on the desk | Priya playing first and working later
test: «done my homework in 5 mins lol now Roblox». What's likely to happen? | It comes back with mistakes to redo | The teacher gives him extra marks | He gets more game time for speed | Nobody checks homework anyway
test: Priya: «I'll do it after, loads of time». What's the risk? | She'll be tired and do it badly | She'll do it better when tired | Homework gets easier later at night | There is no risk if she plays fast
test: Phone buzzing nonstop: «did u see the trailer», «GTA 6 looks sick». Best move for Theo? | Put the phone in another room | Reply to each message quickly | Turn the phone up so he hears | Watch the trailer and then work
test: «miss, my dog ate it». Jayden actually forgot because of game night. What's the better thing to say? | The truth, and offer to do it at lunch | A better excuse about his little brother | Nothing, hope the teacher forgets | That the homework was never set
test: Ruby: «muuum I'm DONE, can I play?» Mum: «When I've checked it.» Why is this useful? | A check catches mistakes before the teacher | Mums just like to be the boss of everybody's homework | Checking makes homework take all night | It is a punishment for being slow
test: Ollie: «I learnt my 7s from a game, no joke». Which is true? | Some games really can help you learn | Games can never teach anything useful | Only board games can teach maths | Learning from a game is a kind of cheating
test: Mia's game says the pyramids were built in a week by giant robots. What should she do for her project? | Check the facts in good books | Copy what the game says exactly | Ask the game to explain it | Leave the pyramids out entirely
test: Mate: «asking your dad for help is for babies». What's true? | Asking for help is clever and saves time | Only babies ever ask for help | You learn nothing at all if you ask someone else | Dads always get homework wrong
test: Big game night planned for Friday. Homework due Monday. What's the smart plan? | Plan when to do it before the game night | Leave it until Monday morning on the bus | Don't do it and say you were ill | Do it during the game night breaks
test: Leo: «homework first then Minecraft, it's kinda motivating». Why does it work? | The reward at the end helps him finish | Minecraft makes his homework shorter | His mum does the hard parts for him | He skips the hard questions to play
test: Zara does her reading on the bus. Teacher: «Is that allowed?» What's the lesson's point? | Finding a time that works for you is smart | Homework must only be done at a desk | The bus is the only place for homework | Reading on a bus never counts as work
exam: Identify the principal consequence of homework completed with undue haste in order to commence play. | It is commonly returned to be completed again | It is invariably awarded full marks | It shortens the following day's lessons | It earns additional time for play
exam: It is given that a pupil's telephone receives frequent messages during the completion of homework. Select the measure most likely to reduce the time required. | The removal of the telephone to another room | The prompt answering of each message received | The increase of the telephone's volume | The completion of homework upon the telephone
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises information concerning history presented in a video game? | It may be altered for excitement and ought to be verified | It is invariably accurate and may be copied directly into a project | It is of no value whatsoever to a pupil | It supersedes the content of reference books
exam: Pursuant to the facts of the lesson, determine the most appropriate conduct of a pupil who has failed to complete homework owing to an evening of play. | To inform the teacher truthfully and complete it promptly | To furnish a fabricated excuse concerning a pet | To copy the homework of a classmate | To remain silent in the hope it is overlooked
